Let me recall the analytical bubble formulas from Qi et al. 2024:

- 1F1B: bubble = (P−1)(Tf + Tb); bubble fraction relative to ideal time M(Tf+Tb) per stage: bubble fraction = (P−1)(Tf+Tb) / (M(Tf+Tb) + (P−1)(Tf+Tb)) = (P−1)/(M+P−1).
- ZB-H1 (which corresponds to ZB-1p roughly): bubble = (P−1)(Tf + Tb − Tw) → since W is off critical path.
- ZB-H2 / ZB-2p: bubble can go to zero (bubble-free upper bound) — with two-pass backward split (B' = input-grad pass, W deferred), bubble → 0 as schedule is "bubble-free" with the "zero bubble" upper bound. Actually the paper: ZB-H2 achieves zero bubble in theory (bubble = 0), with V-type tricks ("ZB-V" for implementation). ZB-2p: split backward into input-grad (B) and weight-grad (W) passes as separate scheduled ops. So the ZB-2p schedule: B' on critical path, W issued later; requires in-flight activation... The paper's "bubble-free" schedule needs the backward pass at stage i to be scheduled with delayed input gradients, requiring an extra microbatch of activation memory and one extra send/recv.

For the design, I don't need perfect fidelity; I define schedule constraints:

1F1B: warmup P forwards at stage 0... standard: in-flight limit P−1; each stage alternates F and B; B_j for microbatch j must run on stage i after B_{j+1} on stage i+1... order constraint.

1F1B-I (eager): forwards issued eagerly (up to memory limit P), backwards ASAP; reduces warmup bubble partially.

ZB-1p: B passes scheduled one stage earlier relative to forwards — i.e., backward of microbatch j at stage i can be issued before forward of microbatch j+something; warmup bubble reduced by removing W from critical path (W is a separate op after B, can be deferred to fill bubbles).

ZB-2p: split B into B' (input gradient, depends on activation from forward of same microbatch one stage below, and incoming grad from stage i+1) and W (weight grad, depends on B' only locally). W can be deferred arbitrarily to fill idle time → near-zero bubble; constraint: grad arrival for B' at stage i comes from B' at stage i+1 (one-pass) — with "2p" maybe the input-gradient pass can be delayed using split? In the paper, ZB-H2 splits backward into two passes to enable bubble-free. ZB-1p keeps backward as single pass (B includes W? no). Let me define:

- Single-pass backward (1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p): B op computes both input grads and weight grads atomically, duration Tb, then W... hmm, actually in the paper they split F and B each into parts too. For the design I'll define clearly:
  - ZB-1p: backward is one pass of duration Tb, but W-work (the last Tw portion) is separable and can be deferred; bubble ≈ (P−1)(Tf+Tb−Tw) roughly... The paper says ZB-H1 bubble = (P−1)(Tf + Tb − Tw)? Let me just present: ZB-1p bubble bound = (P−1)(Tf + Tb − Tw); hmm I recall ZB-H1 = (P−1)(Tf + Tb − Tw)... Actually I believe the paper's Table: 1F1B (P-1)(F+B); ZB-H1 (P-1)(F+B-W); ZB-H2: 0 (with V-shape, "bubble-free upper bound"). I'll present it with the caveat "approximately, per the paper's analysis" and it's a sanity control tolerance.

I'll define the simulator:

- Events per stage: ops (F, B, W) each occupying the stage for its duration; dependencies:
  - F(i, m) requires F(i−1, m) done (i>0), plus F(i, m−1) done? No — F order per stage must be increasing m (no reordering requirement is a design choice; allow out-of-order F if memory allows? In paper, ZB allows B before some F. F per stage must be in microbatch order? Actually forwards must be in order because of data dependency? Forward of microbatch m at stage i depends only on forward of m at stage i−1; stage i could process m out of order but typically in order. Allow in-order F for simplicity — state assumption.)
  - B(i, m) requires B(i+1, m) (gradient from downstream) for i<P−1, and F(i, m) done.
  - W(i, m) requires B(i, m) (or B' for 2p), independent of other stages.
  - 1F1B memory constraint: at most P−1 (or 1?) in-flight: actually 1F1B allows at most P−1 outstanding forwards before warmup completes... standard 1F1B: stage i runs P−i warmup forwards. In-flight activations limit: at most one? No: 1F1B limits in-flight microbatches to P (well, warmup P−i for stage i), giving memory O(P) activations.
- 1F1B-I: eager — B(i,m) issued as early as deps allow, subject to in-flight memory limit (same total activation memory as 1F1B, i.e., at most P in-flight per stage... paper says 1F1B-I uses same memory as 1F1B).
- ZB-1p: memory ≤ 1F1B + small constant; B can be scheduled eagerly with the relaxed ordering; W deferred.
- ZB-2p: B' (input-grad, duration Tb − Tw) and W (Tw) separate; W deferred to fill idle; requires one extra activation slot (in-flight +1).

