.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Research Question and Hypotheses (predictions, not findings)\n\n**Question:** Does throughput for 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p improve as the number of microbatches (M) increases, while ZB-2p maintains high throughput even at small M?\n\n**Predictions to be tested:**\n- **H1 (monotone improvement):** Throughput of 1F1B, 1F1B-I, and ZB-1p is monotonically non-decreasing in M, with diminishing returns approaching an asymptote. Reasoning: their bubble time is a fixed additive cost ~(P\u22121)\u00b7(critical-path work per microbatch) independent of M, so bubble *fraction* ~ (P\u22121)/(M+P\u22121) shrinks as M grows; throughput = M/(warmup+bubble+steady time) rises toward 1/(Tf+Tb) (normalized ideal).\n- **H2 (small-M robustness of ZB-2p):** ZB-2p's throughput at the smallest M (e.g., M=4) is already within a few percent of its own asymptote, and exceeds the other three strategies at that M. Reasoning: ZB-2p splits backward into an input-gradient pass (B\u2032, critical path) and a deferred weight-gradient pass (W), so per-microbatch critical-path work drops to ~Tf+Tb\u2032, and the (P\u22121)-term warmup/bubble cost is largely eliminated by filling idle slots with deferred W \u2014 the paper's \"bubble-free\" property \u2014 which does not require large M to amortize.\n- **H3 (ordering of asymptotes):** At large M, throughput ordering is ZB-2p \u2265 ZB-1p \u2265 1F1B-I \u2265 1F1B, reflecting progressively less work on the critical path (Tb vs Tb\u2212Tw vs Tb\u2032).\n\n## 2. Definitions and Source of Schedule Structures\n\nAll four strategies are defined per the published **\"Zero Bubble Pipeline Parallelism\" (Qi et al., ICLR 2024)**. **No repository-specific variant exists in this workspace** (inspection confirmed no scheduler code), so the paper's semantics are the sole authority:\n\n- **1F1B:** strict one-forward-one-back; stage *i* runs (P\u2212i) warmup forwards, then alternates F and B; backward B(i,m) computes input+weight grads atomically (duration Tb); W follows B immediately and is on the critical path.\n- **1F1B-I (eager 1F1B):** forwards and backwards issued as early as dependencies and the same activation-memory limit as 1F1B permit, shrinking warmup bubbles; otherwise identical op structure.\n- **ZB-1p:** single-pass backward (B), but the weight-gradient portion (W, duration Tw \u2264 Tb) is split off the critical path and may be deferred; backward may be scheduled one stage \"earlier\" relative to forwards than in 1F1B. Analytical bubble bound \u2248 (P\u22121)(Tf+Tb\u2212Tw).\n- **ZB-2p (fixed definition per researcher resolution):** two-pass backward split \u2014 B\u2032 (input-activation gradient pass, duration Tb\u2212Tw, on the critical path, depends on downstream B\u2032) and W (weight-gradient pass, duration Tw, depends only on local B\u2032, freely deferrable). Requires one extra in-flight activation slot vs 1F1B. Enables the paper's near-zero (bubble-free) schedule even at small M.\n\n## 3. Experimental Vehicle: Self-Contained Discrete-Event Simulator\n\nRationale: no repo scheduler exists; the claim concerns schedule structure, so an idealized, deterministic, compute-only event simulator (pure Python, CPU, no dependencies) is sufficient and fully feasible.\n\n**Cost model (fixed requirement):** deterministic Tf, Tb, Tw; Tf = 1, Tb = 2\u00b7Tf; no communication or variance. Communication/memory effects appear only as a clearly-labeled qualitative discussion + optional sensitivity extension (\u00a77.3).\n\n**Ops and dependency rules (shared by all strategies):**\n- F(i,m): requires F(i\u22121,m) done (i>0); stage i processes forwards in microbatch order.\n- B(i,m): requires F(i,m) done and B(i+1,m) done (i < P\u22121); B(P\u22121,m) requires only F(P\u22121,m).\n- For ZB-2p: B\u2032(i,m) same deps as B(i,m) but duration Tb\u2212Tw; W(i,m) requires only B\u2032(i,m) done, independent of other stages.\n- For 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p: W(i,m) requires B(i,m) done (local), duration Tw.\n\n**Scheduling policies (priority rules per strategy), pseudocode:**\n\n```\n# State per stage: ready-op queue; global time-stepped list scheduler.\n# Memory limit L = max in-flight forwards with completed-but-not-backwarded F.\n\ndef simulate(strategy, P, M, Tf, Tb, Tw):\n    t = 0; done = {}  # (op, i, m) -> completion time\n    while not all ops done:\n        for each stage i (parallel across stages):\n            cand = ready_ops(i, t, done)   # deps satisfied, stage idle\n            op = policy[strategy].pick(cand, i, state(i))\n            if op: start it (duration per op type)\n        advance t to next completion\n    makespan T = max completion time over all ops (incl. deferred W)\n    return T\n\npolicy 1F1B:   alternate F,B strictly; warmup: stage i issues (P-i) F first;\n               in-flight limit L = P (standard 1F1B memory); W immediately after B.\npolicy 1F1B-I: eager: pick earliest-ready op, prefer B when B ready (limits\n               in-flight to L = P); removes idle F slots vs 1F1B.\npolicy ZB-1p:  eager like 1F1B-I but W is LOW priority: schedule W only when\n               no F/B is ready (fills idle slots); in-flight limit L = P + 1.\npolicy ZB-2p:  eager on F and B\u2032 (B\u2032 preferred when ready, keeps grad chain\n               hot); W lowest priority, deferred to any idle slot;\n               in-flight limit L = P + 1 (extra activation slot for split).\n```\n\nA greedy event-driven simulation with these priorities reproduces the paper's static timetables for these strategy families; this is what makes the analytical sanity check in \u00a76 meaningful.\n\n## 4. Variables and Grid (fixed requirement)\n\n- **Independent:** strategy \u2208 {1F1B, 1F1B-I, ZB-1p, ZB-2p}; M \u2208 {4, 8, 16, 32, 64, 128}; P \u2208 {4, 8} minimum, P=16 added if cheap (it is \u2014 see \u00a78).\n- **Sensitivity:** Tw \u2208 {Tb/3, Tb/2} (i.e., Tw \u2208 {2/3, 1} when Tf=1, Tb=2).\n- **Controls (fixed requirement):** identical Tf/Tb/Tw and identical (P, M) across all four strategies; warmup included in makespan.\n\n## 5. Measures\n\nPer cell (strategy, P, M, Tw):\n- **Throughput** = M / T (microbatches per unit makespan), also normalized as throughput / (1/(Tf+Tb)) so 1.0 = ideal no-bubble throughput.\n- **Bubble fraction** per stage = 1 \u2212 (useful F+B time)/(stage busy+idle span); report mean across stages and the worst (bottleneck) stage, since throughput is set by the bottleneck.\n- Optional diagnostic: W-fill efficiency for ZB-1p/ZB-2p (fraction of idle slots occupied by deferred W).\n\n## 6. Controls and Validation (fixed requirement)\n\n**Internal sanity control \u2014 simulator vs. analytical formulas** (validates simulator structure, not the hypothesis):\n- 1F1B: bubble fraction \u2248 (P\u22121)/(M+P\u22121). Simulated vs analytical agreement within a small tolerance (e.g., \u22642 percentage points) at all (P, M).\n- ZB-1p: bubble fraction \u2248 (P\u22121)(Tf+Tb\u2212Tw) / (M(Tf+Tb) + (P\u22121)(Tf+Tb\u2212Tw)) \u2014 check against paper's stated bound; tolerance widened (\u22645 pp) since the deferrable-W fill is policy-dependent.\n- ZB-2p: simulated bubble fraction \u2248 0 at moderate/large M (bubble-free upper bound); small positive values tolerated only at very small M where W-fill may be imperfect.\n- 1F1B-I: must lie between 1F1B and ZB-1p (monotonicity check \u2014 structural, formula-free).\nIf any check fails, the schedule generator is corrected before any hypothesis analysis is interpreted.\n\n## 7. Analysis Plan\n\n7.1 **Primary analysis:** For each P and Tw: plot normalized throughput vs M (log-2 x-axis), one curve per strategy. Test H1 by monotonicity of 1F1B/1F1B-I/ZB-1p curves and by the gain ratio throughput(M=128)/throughput(M=4) > 1 with the curve shape consistent with the (P\u22121)/(M+P\u22121) asymptote. Test H2 by ZB-2p's throughput(M=4) \u2265 95% of its own asymptotic throughput and \u2265 each other strategy's throughput at M=4. Test H3 by large-M ordering.\n\n7.2 **Decision criteria (declared a priori):**\n- \"Throughput improves with M\" = monotone non-decreasing in M **and** \u22655% gain from M=4 to M=128.\n- \"Maintains high throughput at fewer microbatches\" = ZB-2p at its smallest tested M is within 5% of its own large-M plateau **and** \u2265 the other strategies' throughput at the same M.\n- Robustness: conclusions must hold for both Tw values and both P values to be reported as supported.\n\n7.3 **Optional labeled extension (not core):** qualitative discussion + optional sensitivity runs adding a fixed per-boundary comm delay or reducing L to test how memory/comm pressure changes the qualitative picture; any such results are reported separately and never used for the primary criteria.\n\n## 8. Feasibility\n\nPure Python, no dependencies, CPU-only, network not needed. Grid size: 4 strategies \u00d7 2\u20133 P \u00d7 6 M \u00d7 2 Tw = 96\u2013144 cells; each simulation is O(P\u00b7M\u00b73) ops with a simple event loop \u2014 total runtime seconds-to-minutes on one core of the 224-core allocation. P=16 is cheap and will be included. Output: CSV of all cells + summary plots + the validation table from \u00a76.\n\n## 9. Fixed Requirements vs. Assumptions\n\n**Fixed requirements (from task + researcher resolutions):** design-only deliverable; ZB-2p = two-pass backward split per Qi et al. 2024, cited as the sole source (no repo variant to deviate from); idealized compute-only model; grid and timing per \u00a74; shared controls per \u00a74; warmup in makespan; throughput + bubble fraction per cell; analytical-formula validation as internal control; expected outcomes framed as predictions.\n\n**Assumptions (mine, consequential):**\n- Greedy priority-based simulation reproduces each strategy's canonical timetable (this is exactly what the \u00a76 validation tests).\n- Deterministic timings: throughput differences are attributable to schedule structure alone; real-system variance is out of scope.\n- Forwards must execute in microbatch order within a 